Have you ever wondered why arrays in C/C++ (and many other languages) start with indexing at 0 instead of 1? Arrays as Contiguous Memory Blocks How arr[i] Works: Pointer Arithmetic Explained Why This Forces Indexing to Start at 0 What If Arrays Started at Index 1? Why arr[i] and i[arr] Mean the Same Thing Conclusion At its core, an array in C/C++ is a fixed-size collection of elements of the same type, stored in contiguous memory locations. When you declare int arr[100];
The compiler allocates space for 100 consecutive integers. An int typically occupies 4 bytes (on 32/64-bit architectures). So, the array consumes 400 bytes, laid out back-to-back in memory. arr[i] Works: Pointer Arithmetic Explained
The real reason arrays start at index 0 has nothing to do with counting or convention. I…
